        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.virginia.org/places-to-visit/regions/chesapeake-bay/white-stone/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from a 1819 church deed that described a boundary “near the road leading by the white stone.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Stone%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pop Castle, a historic plantation home, was bombarded by a Union gunboat during the Civil War and later listed on the National Register in 1989.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Stone%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Epaphroditus Lawson, born in Yorkshire, acquired 4,600 acres along the Rappahannock River in 1649–50, laying the groundwork for White Stone.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Stone%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "White Stone sits in the lower part of Lancaster County, the historic Northern Neck, and has long been tied to agriculture and maritime life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.virginia.org/places-to-visit/regions/chesapeake-bay/white-stone/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today the town hosts a local seafood market and the Allure Art Center, reflecting its ongoing cultural revival.",
